{"id":"column","title":"Infinitely many bounces, in finite time","blurb":"Drop three balls in a line so they land together. If they are bouncy enough they rattle a few times and fly apart. Make them slightly less bouncy and something strange happens: they collide an unbounded number of times in a finite stretch of time, and freeze into a stack.","finding":"The switch is at r = 7 \u2212 4\u221a3 = 0.071797 (McNamara & Young 1992), and the sim resolves it to a millionth: at r = 0.071796 the balls never separate (4000 collisions and still going), while at 0.071798 they are done after 904.","view":[0.02,0,0.3,4200.0],"kind":"curve","axes":["ball-to-ball bounciness r","collisions before they separate"],"mark":{"x":0.071797,"label":"7 \u2212 4\u221a3"},"cap":4000,"series":[{"label":"collisions","pts":[[0.021539,4000],[0.022411,4000],[0.023283,4000],[0.024154,4000],[0.025026,4000],[0.025898,4000],[0.02677,4000],[0.027642,4000],[0.028514,4000],[0.029385,4000],[0.030257,4000],[0.031129,4000],[0.032001,4000],[0.032873,4000],[0.033744,4000],[0.034616,4000],[0.035488,4000],[0.03636,4000],[0.037232,4000],[0.038104,4000],[0.038975,4000],[0.039847,4000],[0.040719,4000],[0.041591,4000],[0.042463,4000],[0.043334,4000],[0.044206,4000],[0.045078,4000],[0.04595,4000],[0.046822,4000],[0.047694,4000],[0.048565,4000],[0.049437,4000],[0.050309,4000],[0.051181,4000],[0.052053,4000],[0.052924,4000],[0.053796,4000],[0.054668,4000],[0.05554,4000],[0.056412,4000],[0.057284,4000],[0.058155,4000],[0.059027,4000],[0.059899,4000],[0.060771,4000],[0.061643,4000],[0.062514,4000],[0.063386,4000],[0.064258,4000],[0.06513,4000],[0.066002,4000],[0.066874,4000],[0.067745,4000],[0.068617,4000],[0.069489,4000],[0.070361,4000],[0.071233,4000],[0.072104,51],[0.072976,26],[0.073848,20],[0.07472,16],[0.075592,14],[0.076464,13],[0.077335,12],[0.078207,11],[0.079079,10],[0.079951,10],[0.080823,9],[0.081694,9],[0.082566,9],[0.083438,8],[0.08431,8],[0.085182,8],[0.086054,8],[0.086925,7],[0.087797,7],[0.088669,7],[0